; ==========================================================================
; 0xF157A8-0xF17558 -- THE DSP EFFECT EDITOR'S VALUE LISTS, 7601 bytes
;
; 18 arrays of interpreter-B display-list records, each followed IMMEDIATELY by
; the string table its records index.  The 36 objects tile this range with no
; gap and no overlap -- that is the check, and it is re-run on every emit.
;
; HOW THE FIRMWARE GETS HERE.  0xF132E4 is a 32-entry array of pointers, one per
; editor screen -- the third of four parallel 32-entry arrays sharing a row index
; (0xF131E4, 0xF13264, 0xF132E4, 0xF13364; one reference each, at 0xF10700,
; 0xF110EA, 0xF110FA, 0xF1172A).  0xF110FA fetches the row's array, adds a byte
; offset and calls slot 0xF42E0C = T_DisplayListB_RunOne_Stack (0xF3183D), which sets
; XIX = XIY+1 so interpreter B draws EXACTLY ONE record.  Records are therefore
; addressed individually, and every one is padded with 0xFF to a 15-byte STRIDE
; so that `base + 15*line` reaches each of them.  Rows 0 and 31 of the 0xF132E4
; array both hold 0x00F157A8 -- its default value, matching rows 0 and 31 of the
; other three arrays, which both hold their own default 0x00F42C70.
;
; RECORD LAYOUT (interpreter B, notes/FINDINGS-ui-display-list-interpreter-b.md):
;   +0 opcode   +1 length   +2 source variable   +4 AND mask   +5 right shift
;   +6 swi 7 function   then, for opcode 02: +7 string table, +0x0B bytes per
;   entry, +0x0D -> IX; for opcodes 00/05/06: +7 -> IX, +9 digit count.
; All 176 records here read the same staging byte, RAM 0x2640, which the caller
; writes at 0xF110AC immediately before running the record.
;
; ⚠ ENTRY COUNTS come from each table's EXTENT, not from its record's mask.  The
; mask gives (mask >> shift) + 1, an upper bound: 0xF15A7C's record says 32 and
; the table is 27 entries.  Every extent below divides exactly by the width the
; record passes as BC.
; Regenerate + re-check: python3 notes/gen_prom_b_dsp_value_lists.py --selftest
; ==========================================================================

; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; DLB_Records_F157A8 -- 16 interpreter-B records at a 15-byte stride,
;   0xF157A8-0xF15897.  Opcode 0x02.
; Called from: 0xF132E4 -- the entry of the 0xF132E4 screen array that
;   names it.  The consumer described in this block's header fetches that
;   entry, adds `15 * line`, and runs ONE record.
; Inputs:  RAM 0x2640, written immediately before the call.
; Outputs: one drawn field per call.
; Evidence: every record's length byte equals the length its interpreter-B
;   handler implies, and every byte between a record's end and its stride
;   boundary is 0xFF.  The 16 records fill 0xF157A8-0xF15897 exactly.
; Structure: 2 screen groups of 8 lines.  Within a group the +0x0D
;   position steps by a constant 0x280 -- 0x0BAA, 0x0E2A, 0x10AA, 0x132A, 0x15AA, 0x182A, 0x1AAA, 0x1D2A -- and it RESETS at
;   each group boundary, which is what makes the group the unit.
; ✅ HALF-ANSWERED 2026-08-31 (wave 8).  This block used to end `Unknown:
;   which effect parameter occupies which line, and -- where there is more
;   than one group -- what selects between them.  The caller adds a byte
;   offset, so `15 * (8*group + line)` reaches any of them, but nothing
;   converted here computes that offset.`  The offset IS computed, in
;   DspEffect_PaintParamEditor (0xF11057): H runs 0, 15, 30 ... 105
;   (`add H,0x0F` at 0xF1115F, `cp H,0x69` at 0xF11165), so a screen is
;   EIGHT lines and the offset is 15*line.  WHICH array is used is the
;   selected effect's own choice: descriptor byte 4*p+1 of
;   EffectParamDescriptors_F12F24 indexes the 32-entry array at 0xF132E4
;   (`mul BC,(XIZ+0xf5)` / `add XBC,0x00f132e4`, 0xF110E0-0xF11102), and
;   THAT is the array whose record 15*line is run.  So parameter p of the
;   current algorithm picks the value list, and the line is its position
;   on the page.  ⚠ Still open: what selects a GROUP above 0 -- the loop
;   above only ever reaches offset 105.  See
;   notes/FINDINGS-prom_b-dsp-effect-parameters.md sec. 6.
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
